2. A bill of exchange that has been accepted, or the sum contained in it.—3†. The sense in which a word or expression is understood; signification; meaning; acceptation.
{{smaller block|An assertion . . . under the common ''acceptance'' of it not only false but odious.
{{right|''South.''}}}}
{{smaller block|'''Acceptance with God''', in ''theol.'', forgiveness of sins and reception into God’s favor.=''Syn.'' ''Acceptance, Acceptancy, Acceptation.'' See ''acceptation.''}}

'''acceptancy''' (ak-sep′tan-si), ''n.'' The act of accepting; acceptance; willingness to receive or accept.
{{smaller block|<poem>Here’s a proof of gift,
But here’s no proof, sir, of ''acceptancy.''</poem>
{{right|''Mrs. Browning'', Aurora Leigh, ii. 1057.}}}}
{{smaller block|='''Syn.''' ''Acceptancy, Acceptance, Acceptation.'' See ''acceptation.''}}

'''acceptant''' (ak-sep′tant), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< F. ''acceptant'', < L. ''acceptan(t-)s'', ppr. of ''acceptare'': see ''accept.''] '''I.''' ''a.'' Receptive. ''N. E. D.''

'''II.''' ''n.'' 1. One who accepts; an accepter. Specifically—2. [''cap.''] One of the French bishops and clergy who accepted the bull Unigenitus, issued in 1713 by Pope Clement XI. against the Jansenists.

'''acceptation''' (ak-sep-tā′shon), ''n.'' [=Sp. ''acceptacion''=Pg. ''aceitação''=It. ''accettazione'', < L. as if *''acceptatio(n-)'', < ''acceptare'', receive: see ''accept.''] 1†. The act of accepting or receiving; reception; acceptance: as, the ''acceptation'' of a trust.
{{smaller block|All are rewarded with like coldness of ''acceptation.''
{{right|''Sir P. Sidney.''}}}}

readings.c2s1:
{{smaller block|This is a faithful saying, and worthy of all ''acceptation'', that Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners.
{{right|1 Tim. i. 15.}}}}
{{smaller block|Some things . . . are notwithstanding of so great dignity and ''acceptation'' with God. {{right|''Hooker'', Eccles. Pol., ii.}}}}
{{smaller block|[Richard Cromwell] spake also with general ''acceptation'' and applause when he made his speech before the Parliament, even far beyond the Lord Fynes.
{{right|''Quoted by Lowell'', Among my Books, 1st ser., p. 261.}}}}
'''3.''' The meaning or sense in which a word or statement is taken or understood: as, this term is to be understood in its usual ''acceptation''.
{{smaller block|Genius is a word which, in common ''acceptation'', extends much further than to the objects of taste. {{right|''H. Blair'', Lect.}}}}
{{smaller block|= '''Syn.''' ''Acceptance'', ''Acceptancy'', ''Acceptation''. These words have been used interchangeably, but there is a marked tendency to use ''acceptance'' for the act of accepting, and ''acceptation'' for the state of being accepted, ''acceptancy'' having become rare, or being restricted to poetic use.}}
{{smaller block|It is in vain to stand out against the full ''acceptance'' of a word which is supported by so much and so respectable authority. {{right|''Whitney'', Lang. and Study of Lang., p. 41.}}}}
{{smaller block|To reanimate this drooping but Divine truth of human regeneration, by lifting it out of its almost wholly lapsed and lifeless — because merely ritual — private ''acceptation'', and giving it a grander public application.
{{right|''H. James'', Subs. and Shad., p. 154.}}}}

'''accepted''' (ak-sep′ted), ''p. a.'' '''1.''' Acceptable; chosen; appointed.
{{smaller block|Behold, now is the ''accepted'' time; behold, now is the day of salvation. {{right|2 Cor. vi. 2.}}}}
'''2.''' In ''com.'', received or acknowledged as binding: often abbreviated to ''a.'' or ''A.'' See ''accept-''

