¶
{{smaller block|Our justification which comes by Christ is by imputation and ''acceptilation'', by grace and favour.
{{right|''Jer. Taylor'', Ans. to Bp. of Rochester.}}}}

'''acception†''' (ak-sep′shọn), ''n.'' [< ME. ''accepcioun'', < OF. ''acception'' = Sp. ''acepcion'' = Pg. ''accepção'', < L. ''acceptio(n-)'', < ''accipere'', receive: see ''accept''.] 1. Acceptation.
{{smaller block|The diverse ''acceptions'' of words which the schoolmen call suppositions effect no homonymy.
{{right|''Burgersdicius'', trans. by a Gentleman, I. xxvi. 12.}}}}
{{smaller block|That this hath been esteemed the due and proper ''acception'' of this word, I shall testify.
{{right|''Hammond'', Fundamentals.}}}}
2. The act of favoring unequally; preference.
{{smaller block|For ''accepciouns'' of persoones, that is, to putte oon bifore another withoute desert, is not anentis God.
{{right|''Wyclif'', Rom. ii. 11.}}}}

'''acceptive†''' (ak-sep′tiv), ''a.'' Ready to accept.
{{smaller block|The people generally are very ''acceptive'' and apt to applaud any meritable work.
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Case is Altered, ii. 7.}}}}

'''acceptor''' (ak-sep′tôr or -tėr), ''n.'' [After L. ''acceptor'', one who receives, < ''accipere'', receive: see ''accept'', ''v.''] Same as ''accepter'', but more frequent in commercial and legal use.—'''Acceptor supra protest''', {{smaller|a person, not a party to a bill of exchange which has been protested, who accepts it for the honor of the drawer or of an indorser, thereby agreeing to pay it if the drawee does not.}}

'''acceptress''' (ak-sep′tres), ''n.'' A female acceptor. [Rare.]

'''accerse†''' (ak-sėrs′), ''v. t.'' [< L. ''accersere'', commonly ''arcessere'' (prefix ''ar-'', < ''ad-'', to), summon, cause to come, < ''accedere'', come: see ''accede''.]

readings.c3s1:
To call out or forth; summon, as an army. ''Hall.'' [Rare.]

'''access''' (ak′ses, formerly ak-ses′), ''n.'' [< ME. ''acces'', ''aksis'', ''axes'' (nearly always in sense 5), < OF. ''acces'' (also spelled ''aces'', ''acex'', ''aches'', ''axces''), approach, attack, F. ''accès'' = Sp. ''acceso'' = Pg. It. ''accesso'', < L. ''accessus'', approach, passage, increase, < ''accedere'', go to: see ''accede''.] 1. A coming to; near approach; admittance; admission: as, to gain ''access'' to a prince.
{{smaller block|We are denied ''access'' unto his person.
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. IV., iv. 1.}}}}
2. Means of approach or admission; way of entrance or passage to anything: as, the ''access'' is through a massive door or a long corridor, or by a neck of land.
{{smaller block|All ''access'' was throng’d. ''Milton'', P. L., i. 761.
Then closed her ''access'' to the wealthier farms.
{{right|''Tennyson'', Aylmer’s Field.}}}}
3. Admission to sexual intercourse.
{{smaller block|During coverture ''access'' of the husband shall be presumed, unless the contrary be shown. ''Blackstone.''}}
4. Addition; increase; accession.
{{smaller block|<poem>I, from the influence of thy looks, receive
''Access'' in every virtue. ''Milton'', P. L., ix. 310.</poem>}}
5. The attack or return of a fit or paroxysm of disease, as of a fever; accession.
{{smaller block|<poem>Every wight gan waxen for ''accesse''
A leche anon. ''Chaucer'', Troilus, ii. 1578.</poem>
The first ''access'' looked like an apoplexy.
{{right|''Bp. Burnet'', Hist. of Own Times.}}
The most efficient and certain means for stimulating the cerebral cortex, in order to provoke an epileptic ''access'', is electrization. ''Alien. and Neurol.'', VI. 8.}}

